# veh-cluster corpus notes

The ridl reference **Appendix A**, compiled. `cluster/appendix-a.ridl` is the
appendix text verbatim — no line is adapted for the corpus — so every
diagnostic it draws is a fact about the reference, recorded below rather than
edited away. `common/` supplies the `veh.common` vocabulary Appendix A imports;
it is the typl reference Appendix B example, the same text the `veh-common`
entry compiles standalone.

`cluster/services.ridl` is authored on top: Appendix A declares no `service`,
and §14.5 needs both service forms exercised.

## What the entry covers

- **Both service forms.** Two named-reference services over one interface shape
  (`veh.cluster.status` and `veh.cluster.standby` both realize `VehicleStatus`)
  and one inline shape (`veh.hvac.cabin`).
- **The inline shape carries the whole surface.** A service's inline shape is
  an `Interface` stored under `Service.shape`, outside `Package.interfaces`, so
  a consumer that walks `package.interfaces` alone skips it silently — the
  shape of five separate E2 defects. `veh.hvac.cabin` therefore holds all five
  interaction kinds, every timing mode, contracts including one that reads
  `result`, an inline `T | E` fallible return, streams in both positions, a
  `reserved` tombstone, a deprecated member, and both `final` forms. The IR,
  Rust and TypeScript snapshots record what each consumer made of them.
- **Every timing mode.** Strict periodic (`@1s`, `@500us`), a range with both
  bounds, lower-bound-only, upper-bound-only, and one untimed interaction whose
  bounds come from the package `[defaults].timing`. The IR carries resolved
  microsecond bounds in every case, with `default_applied` set only on the last.
- **Ordinals with a gap.** `reserved setEcoMode` holds ordinal 8 in the inline
  shape and `reserved resetCounters` holds ordinal 6 in `VehicleStatus`; the
  surviving interactions carry the gap.
- **Cross-package references.** Appendix A's five imports resolve through the
  workspace member, and `ridl.std` types (`Label`, `Message`, `Timestamp`,
  `Version`) resolve without an import.

## Diagnostics the entry draws, and why they are kept

The entry has no errors, so it is a full-pipeline golden. It carries three
warnings, all of them true statements about the source:

- **TYPL-007, unused import `MAX_SPEED`** — Appendix A imports
  `veh.common.MAX_SPEED` and never uses it. A fact about the appendix text.
- **RIDL-308, named result union in query-return position** — Appendix A writes
  `query getFaultPage(filter: DiagFilter): FaultPageResult`. General form §6.1
  made the inline `FaultPage | DiagError` spelling canonical in return position
  after the appendix was written; the E2 close-out already lists §7/§10.1
  adopting the inline form as doc-sync work. The lint firing here is the
  appendix and the general form disagreeing, correctly reported.
- **RIDL-100, untimed event** — deliberate, in `services.ridl`: it is the only
  way to exercise the default-applied path end to end.

## Imports bind at package scope

`services.ridl` imports only `Ratio`, not `Speed` or `Temperature`, because
`appendix-a.ridl` already imports those and imports bind per package, not per
file (ADR-0002 §1: "files within a package are purely organisational").
Repeating an import in a second file of the same package is reported as
TYPL-006, "conflicting import … alias one of the colliding imports" — a message
written for two *different* symbols competing for one local name, which is not
what a repeated identical import is. Recorded as a finding, not worked around.
